<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enmerkar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enmerkar was a legendary king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, listed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> and remembered in Sumerian epic poems as a son of the sun god Utu. The poem Enmerkar and the Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratta">Aratta</a> describes his contest with the distant land of Aratta for precious stones and credits him with inventing writing on clay to send his messages. No contemporary inscription of his has been found.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enmerkar.jpg" alt="Enmerkar" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original photograph: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg)Transparent background: Joel Bellviure · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Priest-king_from_Uruk,_Mesopotamia,_Iraq,_c._3000_BCE._The_Iraq_Museum_(transparent).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
